# Copy to .env — `cp .env.example .env`. Never commit .env.
#
# This file is read by Docker Compose itself, to decide WHERE ON THE HOST each
# volume lives. It is not the application's config: that's backend/.env, which
# Compose loads *into* the containers via `env_file`. Two different files with
# two different jobs — a variable put in the wrong one silently does nothing.
#
# The matching in-container paths (STAGED_FILES_DIR, TORRENT_WORKING_DIR,
# ROM_LIBRARY_DIR, QBITTORRENT_SAVE_PATH, QBITTORRENT_LIBRARY_PATH) live in
# backend/.env and must keep matching the mount targets in docker-compose.yml.
# Nothing here changes them — these set the *other* end of each mount.
#
# Compose decides bind-vs-volume by the shape of the value: a bare name is a
# named volume, anything containing a slash is a bind mount. Every value below
# must therefore be an absolute path or start with ./. A bare name like
# `staged` is read as a volume reference and Compose rejects the project
# ("refers to undefined volume staged") rather than doing what you meant.

# --- Which services to run --------------------------------------------------
# Postgres, Redis and qBittorrent each ship as a container here, but each one
# can be skipped in favour of a server you already run. Remove its name from
# this list and that container is never created; then point the matching
# variable in backend/.env at the real server:
#
#   local-postgres     -> DATABASE_URL
#   local-redis        -> REDIS_URL      (the only one needed; Celery, Channels
#                                         and the cache derive from it)
#   local-qbittorrent  -> QBITTORRENT_HOST
#
# Note the two files: this list is Compose's own config and lives here, while
# the connection URLs are application config and live in backend/.env. A switch
# means editing both.
#
# Two things that catch people out:
#   - "localhost" inside a container is the container. A server running on this
#     machine is host.docker.internal (Docker Desktop) or the host's LAN
#     address — redis://localhost:6379/0 will not reach it.
#   - Removing a profile doesn't remove an already-running container; it's left
#     behind as an orphan. Use `docker compose up -d --remove-orphans`.
#
# qBittorrent has a further catch — see docs/installation.md.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=local-postgres,local-redis,local-qbittorrent

# Host ports the bundled Postgres and Redis publish on, for reaching them from
# outside Docker (running Django on the host — see docs/development.md). Change
# these if something else on your machine already owns 5434/6380. They have no
# effect on a server you've profiled out: that one's port comes from the
# DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL you point at it.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5434
# REDIS_PORT=6380

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Where downloaded ROM sets land, and the only variable here with no default:
# the stack refuses to start until it's set. Must be an ABSOLUTE path to a
# folder that ALREADY EXISTS (unlike the optional ones below, this one is not
# auto-created), and if it's a network share it must be mounted before `up`.
#
# Point it at whatever you actually want to browse: a NAS share, an external
# disk, the folder your cabinet reads over SMB.
ROM_LIBRARY_HOST_PATH=/absolute/path/to/your/roms

# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave these commented out and each stays a Docker-managed named volume,
# exactly as before this file existed. That's a fine default — it just means
# the files live inside the Docker VM, so on macOS Finder can't reach them and
# `docker compose cp` is the only way to get anything out. Uncomment one to
# put it on a host folder you can actually open. Docker creates the folder if
# it's missing.
#
# Changing one of these after the stack has been running does NOT migrate the
# existing contents — the new location starts empty and whatever was in the
# named volume stays there, still visible under `docker volume ls`.

# Single-game downloads, held here until claimed and then swept on the
# STAGED_FILE_RETENTION_HOURS clock. The one most worth pointing at a real
# folder if you want to grab files without going through the browser.
# STAGED_FILES_HOST_PATH=./data/staged

# Torrent working directory. Shared by Django/Celery and the qBittorrent
# daemon, which see the same files at /app/data/torrents and /downloads
# respectively — this single variable moves both, so they cannot drift apart.
# TORRENT_DATA_HOST_PATH=./data/torrents

# qBittorrent's own config, including its WebUI password. Worth binding if you
# want to back it up or edit it by hand; see docs/installation.md for why
# losing this volume breaks authentication until you reset the password.
# QBITTORRENT_CONFIG_HOST_PATH=./data/qbittorrent

# The Postgres data directory. Bind this only if you have a specific reason —
# on macOS a bind mount puts the database on a virtiofs share, which is
# markedly slower than the named volume for Postgres' access pattern.
# POSTGRES_DATA_HOST_PATH=./data/postgres

# Permissions note: the qbittorrent container runs as PUID/PGID 1000, so on a
# Linux host anything you bind for TORRENT_DATA_HOST_PATH or
# QBITTORRENT_CONFIG_HOST_PATH must be writable by uid 1000. On Docker Desktop
# (macOS/Windows) the file sharing layer handles ownership and this doesn't
# come up.
